.Dd September 13, 2026
.Dt CLODE 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm clode
.Nd build a standalone Claude Code binary for your machine
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Cm build
.Op Cm quaude | naude
.Op Fl -target Ar platform-arch
.Op Fl -out Ar path
.Nm
.Cm fetch
.Cm claude | node
.Op Cm stable | latest | Ar version
.Op Fl -target Ar platform-arch
.Nm
.Cm read-anthropic-tea-leaves
.Nm
.Fl -help
.Nm
.Fl -version
.Nm
.Fl -verbose
.Pp
From a source checkout only:
.Pp
.Dl node scripts/stage0.mjs Cm bootstrap Oo Fl -target Ar platform-arch Oc Oo Fl -out Ar path Oc
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is a builder, not a runner: it fetches an upstream
.Xr claude 1
binary (from a provider package or your own install) and BUILDS a
standalone target from it.
.Nm
itself never executes Claude Code \(en the target it builds does.
.Pp
.Nm Cm build
extracts the upstream JavaScript bundle and builds it into a self-contained
binary that needs nothing else at run time \(en no Node, no npm, no network.
There are two such targets, differing in engine and in where each can be
built, not in what they need at run time.
By default,
.Nm Cm build
compiles the bundle to bytecode for a pinned
.Xr tjs 1
(QuickJS) runtime, producing
.Dq quaude ;
because tjs is portable,
.Nm
can build a quaude on a machine that has never had Node installed.
.Nm Cm build Cm naude
instead bakes the bundle into a Node SEA
(single executable application), called
.Dq naude :
a copy of the build machine's own Node binary with the bundle embedded, so
a built naude is just as self-contained at run time as a quaude.
Building one needs a real Node
.Ar >= 24
to copy from, so naude can only be built where Node already exists; it
serves as quaude's higher-fidelity twin \(en real Node built-ins, and the
reference side of the shim's Node-parity checks.
.Nm
never installs, names, or replaces anything called
.Ar claude ;
it reads the upstream binary only as the source of the bundle and never
redistributes it.
Both quaude and naude carry Anthropic's code and are derived work: each is
built locally, on your machine, and never distributed.
.Pp
.Fl -version
prints clode's own version and exits, and
.Fl -help
prints clode's own options and exits.
.Fl -verbose
(or
.Ev CLODE_VERBOSE Ns = Ns Cm 1 )
un-mutes clode's own progress, such as bundle extraction and dependency
installation.
The flag is recognized only in the leading position.
Errors are always shown regardless.
.Pp
.Nm Cm fetch
takes the INGREDIENT to fetch:
.Cm claude ,
the upstream binary a quaude is built from, or
.Cm node ,
the pinned Node a naude embeds.
The ingredient is required \(em there is no default.
.Pp
.Nm Cm fetch Cm claude
.Op Cm stable | latest | Ar version
fetches an upstream Claude Code build matching this machine's OS into clode's
provider store and verifies its checksum.
This is the build's input step:
.Nm Cm build
does not fetch on its own \(em it resolves an already-present provider and
fails with a pointer to
.Nm Cm fetch Cm claude
if none is found.
It does not take
.Fl -target :
the provider store is keyed by version alone, so a foreign-OS fetch would
replace this machine's provider; use
.Ev CLODE_FETCH_PLATFORM
to choose the upstream build deliberately.
With no release argument the channel is taken from the
.Cm autoUpdatesChannel
setting in
.Pa managed-settings.json
or
.Pa $CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR/settings.json
(default
.Pa ~/.claude/settings.json ) ,
falling back to
.Cm latest
\(em matching Claude Code's own default.
.Pp
.Nm Cm fetch Cm node
.Op Fl -target Ar platform-arch
fetches the pinned Node
.Nm Cm build Cm naude
embeds, sha-verified, into a per-platform store, so a naude can be built on a
machine with no Node installed.
Unlike the provider,
.Nm Cm fetch Cm node
does take
.Fl -target :
the store holds one Node per platform, which is what a naude cross-build needs.
.Pp
.Nm Cm build
.Op Fl -out Ar path
builds a standalone
.Dq quaude
binary on this machine (default
.Pa ./quaude ) :
the pinned txiki.js runtime plus the extracted Claude Code bundle compiled to
quickjs bytecode, self-contained (no Node, no npm, no network at run time).
The build verifies itself before reporting success \(em a canned offline
Messages round-trip and a
.Fl -clode-attest
manifest verification \(em and fails loudly otherwise.
The built binary reserves only the
.Fl -clode-*
option namespace
.Pq currently Fl -clode-attest
for itself and passes everything else to Claude Code \(en quaude is the one
that executes Claude Code and needs a passthrough namespace;
.Nm
itself no longer does.
Running a built binary with
.Fl -clode-attest
re-hashes everything it carries, prints its build manifest \(em which
.Nm
built it, which Claude Code bundle it embeds, and which platform that bundle
was carved for \(em and exits nonzero if anything fails to verify.
The same flag, output format and verdict line apply to a
.Dq naude
.Pq below .
The template runtime comes from
.Ev CLODE_TJS
(default
.Pa build/tjs/tjs
under the clode root).
.Pp
.Nm Cm build Cm naude
.Op Fl -out Ar path
bundles Claude Code as a Node SEA instead of a quaude: the extracted bundle is
baked into a self-contained binary built from the host Node, and runs with no
Node installed, exactly like a quaude.
Build machines only \(em the SEA build pipeline requires Node
.Ar >= 24
on the build machine, not on the machine that runs the result.
Like a quaude, a naude carries Anthropic's code and is derived work: it is
built locally and never distributed.
.Pp
A Claude Code binary built by
.Nm
cannot update itself in place: its bundle is baked into its own bytecode.
Where an npm-installed Claude Code's in-app updater would fetch and apply a
new version, a quaude or naude's patched updater callback refuses instead
of lying about success; take a new version by running
.Nm Cm fetch Cm claude
then
.Nm Cm build
again.
.Pp
.Nm Cm read-anthropic-tea-leaves
runs one check now for upstream changes that threaten clode's ability to
repackage Claude Code \(en either a break in the shim's correctness or a
Bun/Node API clode does not yet cover \(em prints a summary, and exits.
.Nm Cm build
also performs this check opportunistically, at most once per day, fully in
the background, and prints a one-line notice on the next
.Nm Cm build
only when a newer version carries such signals.
There is no launch to hook it to anymore, so
.Nm Cm build
is where the check runs.
The name states the command's epistemic status: it INFERS Anthropic's
direction of travel from a changelog, is never authoritative, and never
downloads anything.
.Pp
.Cm bootstrap
builds
.Nm
ITSELF from a source checkout \(em the builder, not a Claude Code target \(em
and is the one verb a downloaded
.Nm
does not have: asked to bootstrap, it says so and exits 2.
It is release plumbing (the CI builder legs and the cross-blobulate guests run
.Li "node scripts/stage0.mjs bootstrap" ) ,
takes the same
.Fl -target
and
.Fl -out
as a product build, and defaults its output to
.Pa ./clode-native .
The esbuilt clode-main bundle it embeds comes from
.Ev CLODE_MAIN_BUNDLE
.Pq default: the newest Pa build/*/clode-main.bundle.cjs ,
built by
.Li "node scripts/build-clode-main.mjs" .
.Pp
The
.Nm
you download is itself a native, self-contained binary \(en no
.Xr node 1 ,
no npm, nothing else at run time; it builds a quaude on a machine that has
never heard of Node.
Running
.Nm
from a source checkout is the other shape:
.Pa scripts/stage0.mjs
is a Node program
.Pq Li #!/usr/bin/env node
and needs
.Xr node 1
on
.Ev PATH
.Ar >= 20 .
An ES5-safe prologue prints a friendly
.Dq node too old
message on an outdated node; a truly missing node yields
.Li "env: node: not found" .
Either shape,
.Nm Cm build Cm naude
needs Node
.Ar >= 24
on the host, because a Node SEA is built from a real Node.
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width CLODE_CHANGELOG_URL
.It Ev CLODE_VERBOSE
When non-empty, un-mutes clode's own progress output (same as
.Fl -verbose ) .
.It Ev CLODE_CLAUDE_BIN
Absolute path to the upstream claude binary to build from (highest
precedence).
.It Ev CLODE_VERSION_DIR
Absolute path to the claude binary of a specific already-fetched version to
build from; checked after
.Ev CLODE_CLAUDE_BIN
and before the provider's
.Cm current
pointer.
.It Ev CLODE_PROVIDERS
Store for binaries fetched by
.Nm Cm fetch Cm claude
(default
.Pa ${XDG_DATA_HOME:-$HOME/.local/share}/clode/providers ) .
.It Ev CLODE_RELEASES_URL
Base URL for
.Nm Cm fetch Cm claude
downloads.
.It Ev CLODE_FETCH_PLATFORM
Which platform's build
.Nm Cm fetch Cm claude
fetches (default: this machine's OS).
.It Ev CLODE_NO_WATCH
If set, disable the opportunistic drift check that
.Nm Cm build
fires.
.It Ev CLODE_WATCH_INTERVAL
Seconds between opportunistic checks (default 86400).
.It Ev CLODE_WATCH_DIR
Directory for watcher state (throttle + notice); default
.Pa ${XDG_CACHE_HOME:-$HOME/.cache}/clode .
.It Ev CLODE_CHANGELOG_URL
Release-notes source the watcher scans for repackaging-impacting signals.
.It Ev CLODE_CACHE
Cache root (default
.Pa ${XDG_CACHE_HOME:-$HOME/.cache}/clode ) .
.It Ev CLODE_LIBEXEC
Directory holding the extractor and Bun shim.
.It Ev CLODE_TJS
The txiki.js template binary
.Nm Cm build
builds from (default
.Pa build/tjs/tjs
under the clode root).
.It Ev CLODE_NODE
Host node (absolute path).
.It Ev CLODE_MAIN_BUNDLE
The esbuilt clode-main bundle
.Cm bootstrap
embeds (default: the newest
.Pa build/*/clode-main.bundle.cjs ) .
.It Ev CLODE_TARGET_TEMPLATE
An operator-built engine to cross-build onto, used INSTEAD of the published
template for
.Fl -target .
Read by both
.Nm Cm build Cm quaude Fl -target
and
.Cm bootstrap Fl -target ;
.Nm Cm build Cm naude
never looks at it.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa LIBEXEC/extract-claude-js.cjs , Pa LIBEXEC/bun-shim.cjs
Extractor and Bun-global shim.
.It Pa ${XDG_CACHE_HOME:-$HOME/.cache}/clode/<key>/cli.cjs
Per-binary extracted bundle cache.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr claude 1 ,
.Xr node 1
.Sh AUTHORS
.An Amitai Schleier
